/**

 @page tutorials_multigrid_matrixfree_discontinuous_galerkin_poisson Matrix-free Discontinuous Galerkin for the Poisson equation

 This simple example discusses the steps from the file t.b.d..
 We omit some technical details (such as the parsing of the command line) and 
 focus on key ingredients. Therefore, it is reasonable to study this description 
 in combination with the actual script. It is best to read this documentation in 
 combination with the docu of the class multigrid.matrixfree.solvers.api.DiscontinuousGalerkinDiscretisationPointWiseRiemannSolver,
 which provides a lot of details on the actual implementation and mathematical ingredients.
 This page is more on the how to use the class to construct a working solver.
 
 Once again, we solve the Poisson equation, and employ a Discontinuous Galerkin
 formalism with a point-wise Riemann solver. That is, we start from
 
 \f{eqnarray*}{
 - \Delta u & = & f \\
 \int \left( - \Delta u, \phi \right) dx & = & \int (f, \phi) dx \\
 \int _c \left( \nabla u, \nabla \phi \right) dx - \sum _f \int _f \left( \nabla u, n \right) \phi dS(x) & = & \int (f, \phi) dx \\
 \int _c \left( \nabla u, \nabla \phi \right) dx - \sum _f \int _f \left( \mathcal{R}(u^-,u^+), n \right) \phi dS(x) & = & \int (f, \phi) dx
 \f}
 
 which leads to the equation system

 @f$
  A u|_c - P \mathcal{R}(u^-,u^+) = A u|_c - P \mathcal{R}(u|_f) = A u|_c - P \varphi|_f = Mf
  @f$  
 
 per cell.
 
 
 # Project setup
 
 To be written.
 
 We create a solver of the type multigrid.matrixfree.solvers.api.DiscontinuousGalerkinDiscretisationPointWiseRiemannSolver.
 This solver is given the name MyDG in the present example.
 
 # Implement core
 
 Upon the first run of the Python script, we get two new C++ classes, i.e. four
 files: AbstractMyDG.h, AbstractMyDG.cpp, MyDG.h, MyDG.cpp. The interesting file
 for the user is MyDG.cpp
 
 
 
*/
